# Testing Standards

## Evidence for Reviews and Improvements

- Apply this policy to reviews and improvements in every executable layer,
  including Dart, Rust, FFI, hooks, scripts, and build or release automation.
- Identify the affected behavior, invariant, or contract and the checks that
  demonstrate it before changing code. Preserve public contracts and ABI unless
  the task explicitly authorizes changing them.
- Every behavioral or structural improvement must have relevant automated
  tests demonstrating correctness or preserved behavior. Run existing tests
  when they already prove the claim; add or extend tests for uncovered cases.
  Do not add tests that only mirror implementation details.
- A review without code changes must report its scope, evidence, findings, and
  gaps. Reading code alone is not proof of correctness, full review coverage,
  or a performance gain; do not invent tests for a review with no new behavior.
- For rule-only or documentation-only edits, validate references, consistency,
  and diff hygiene. Run behavioral tests if executable behavior is affected.
- Report exact commands, tested revision or diff, results, and skipped or
  blocked checks. Keep unvalidated claims explicitly pending; compilation,
  static analysis, and a passing unrelated suite do not replace targeted tests.

## Performance Evidence

- Every claimed performance improvement requires correctness tests and a
  reproducible before/after benchmark of the affected path. Keep the benchmark
  in the repository so the comparison can be repeated.
- Compare an identified baseline and candidate with identical benchmark code,
  inputs, build profile, features, toolchain, and environment. Measure optimized
  builds; disclose any unavoidable differences that limit comparability.
- Exercise representative valid inputs, including small and large workloads
  and relevant variants such as compression. Check affected neighboring paths
  and previously optimized cases for regressions, not only the best case.
- Validate outputs outside timed sections and prevent unused work from being
  optimized away. Use warm-up and repeated samples; do not run competing
  benchmarks concurrently on the same machine.
- Report the metric and units, baseline and candidate values, relative change,
  sample count, variability or confidence interval, and reproduction commands.
  Do not claim a gain from a single timing or a difference within measurement
  noise; mark inconclusive measurements as inconclusive.
- Investigate measured regressions and resolve them or disclose the trade-off
  before declaring the improvement complete. Benchmarks do not replace tests,
  and correctness tests alone do not prove speed or memory improvements.
- Keep timing-sensitive measurements separate from deterministic correctness
  tests; use the repository's performance guards where appropriate.

## Structure

- Mirror Dart production folders in `test/`; use Rust inline unit tests and
  crate integration tests as described in `rust_testing.mdc`.
- Group tests by observable behavior, not by private implementation details.
- Keep test file names predictable and easy to map back to source files.

## Test Design

- Follow AAA: Arrange, Act, Assert.
- Keep tests deterministic and isolated.
- Use descriptive names: `should_<behavior>_when_<condition>`.
- Validate behavior, not implementation details.
- Keep assertions close to the behavior they validate.

## Test Levels

- Unit tests validate small pure rules, mappers, entities, helpers, and typed
  errors.
- Service or component tests validate collaboration between nearby parts such as
  service and repository boundaries.
- Integration tests validate real contracts with FFI, native code, assets, or
  environment-dependent resources.
- E2E tests validate live database or driver behavior under controlled external
  configuration.

## Mocks and Fakes

- Mock external boundaries only, such as DB, FFI, process, network, or platform
  dependencies.
- Prefer real domain entities and value objects in unit tests.
- Use fakes when behavior matters more than interaction verification.
- Do not mock pure logic without a concrete reason.

## Failure Paths

- Prioritize critical paths and error handling.
- Cover boundary conditions and null or empty inputs.
- Validate expected failure behavior, not just that "an error happened."

## Confirmed Bug Regression

- Add a regression test in the same change as the fix for every confirmed bug.
- Cover the actual failing input or sequence and assert the expected behavior,
  not merely absence of an exception. Include valid-path and boundary cases
  needed to show that the fix preserves existing contracts.
- Run the regression against the broken behavior first when feasible, then
  verify it passes with the fix. Report when the failing baseline cannot be run.
- Keep the test in the suite so a returning failure is detected.
- Do not mark a bug fixed without its regression test passing. If prerequisites
  block execution, report the fix as pending validation; inability to run the
  failing baseline does not waive the regression-test requirement.
- Name the test after the broken behavior:
  `should_<behavior>_when_<condition>`.
- Use the simplest level that reproduces the bug: unit, integration, or E2E.

## Environment-Dependent Tests

- Gate live or environment-dependent tests clearly.
- Skip optional environment-driven tests with explicit messages when
  prerequisites are missing. If a run explicitly requires that environment,
  report missing prerequisites as failed or blocked validation, not success.
- Do not convert assertion failures into skips or weaken assertions to pass.
- Keep helpers for env loading and test setup centralized.

## Review Checklist

- [ ] Tests mirror the relevant source structure.
- [ ] Each test covers one observable behavior contract.
- [ ] External dependencies are isolated correctly.
- [ ] Critical and error paths are covered.
- [ ] The simplest test level that proves the behavior was chosen.
- [ ] Environment-dependent tests fail or skip clearly.
- [ ] Every confirmed bug fixed in the change has a regression test.
- [ ] Each improvement has targeted correctness or behavior-preservation evidence.
- [ ] Performance claims have reproducible before/after benchmark evidence.
- [ ] Review scope, results, and validation gaps are reported accurately.
